# Staff costs over time
# Monthly salary per person for each enumerated staff type, in Simucents.
# The engine charges the salary per person per month, applies
# calc_adjusted_monthly_figure (x2 at bits_per_month=22, meters_per_tile=125), and
# interpolates linearly between the listed years, clamping at both ends.
#
# Basis (2026-11-23 v2): nominal annual wages in OLD pence (240 to the pound).
# staff[N] value = annual old pence x 10/3.
# Grades 0-6 are the land grades; 7-9 the sea grades and 10-11 the air grades, added in v4
# (2026-11-23). The sea and air basis and sources are in the v4 block at the end of this file.
# Derivation: 1 old penny = 80 Simucents; annual / 12 = monthly Simucents; the engine's
# monthly factor doubles the charge, so store half: 80/12/2 = 10/3.
# The stored value must include this conversion: without it the engine charges 3/10 of
# the annual figure. Each grade has its own line.
#
# Sources (full register and measured tables: New/ai/staff-wages.md and its children):
# - 1710-1911 general labourer: Williamson (1982) via Alan Stanier, "Relative Value of
#   Sums of Money" (archived 2011).
# - 1906-1907 grades: Board of Trade, Report of an Enquiry ... IV. Public Utility
#   Services in 1906 (Cd. 5196); VII. Railway Service in 1907 (Cd. 6053), in the
#   Fifteenth Abstract of Labour Statistics (Cd. 6514, 1913).
# - 1914-1961 standard time rates: Hansard, HC Deb 2 February 1962, vol. 652,
#   cols. 132-138W; 20 June 1922 (vol. 155, col. 1049); 30 July 1925 (vol. 187,
#   cols. 671-3W).
# - 1962-1968: Department of Employment and Productivity, British Labour Statistics:
#   Historical Abstract 1886-1968 (1971), Table 50, applied to each grade's 1961
#   differential.
# - 1946 and 1885-1922 grades: Simutrans forum topic 6521 (see staff-wages.md S12).
# - 1980-1995: ONS adhoc 005457, New Earnings Survey, Table 86 (full-time men on adult
#   rates, mean gross weekly earnings excluding those whose pay was affected by
#   absence). Years read: 1980, 1982, 1985, 1988, 1990, 1992, 1995. Locomotive
#   drivers and motormen 115.20 / 151.70 / 191.70 / 248.50 / - / - / 386.90; bus and
#   coach drivers 117.40 / 143.60 / 171.70 / 191.40 / 216.40 / 234.70 / 243.50;
#   general labourers 94.00 / 113.10 / 134.00 / 166.30, then other building and civil
#   engineering labourers 205.90 / 233.80 / 250.20; rail transport inspectors,
#   supervisors and guards 323.50 in 1995.
# - 1999-2026: ONS Annual Survey of Hours and Earnings, Table 14.7a annual gross pay,
#   all employees, median, UK (1999-2016 from the ONS 4-digit occupational timeseries,
#   adhoc 006334; 2017-2026 from the annual Table 14.7a releases).
#
# Method: measured points are listed; the unmeasured years (1981, 1983-84, 1986-87,
# 1989, 1991, 1993-94, 1996-98) are filled by the engine's interpolation between them.
# No rail driver row was published in 1990 or 1992, so the driver is interpolated
# 1988-1995. 1999-2026 is measured annually. 2030-2050 holds 2026 real pay and rises
# with the general index in prices.tab (16281 in 2026). The measured/derived/estimated
# status of every point is in New/ai/staff-wages/reconstruction.md and
# New/ai/staff-wages/post-1980.md.
#
# 2026-11-23 v2: the 1980 anchor was replaced. The previous figures (driver 142.77,
# bus driver 99.94, labourer 85.66 a week) were of unverified provenance and did not
# match the New Earnings Survey. The labourer is now ASHE elementary construction
# occupations, not farm workers. Every stored value converts back to the intended
# annual figure (stored x 3/10 / 240 = annual pounds).
# 2026-11-23 v3: the 1980-1999 constant-rate bridge was replaced by the measured NES
# years 1982, 1985, 1988, 1990, 1992 and 1995; see staff-wages/post-1980.md section 3.

