# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-11-23 v4: sea and air crew grades (7-11).
#
# These five grades extend the file from the seven land grades. The complement
# declared on each vehicle is in New/ai/staff-wages/crew-complements.md; the
# evidence and the measured/derived/estimate status of every point are in
# New/ai/staff-wages/crew-complements/ships.md, aircraft.md and their child
# registers and source tables.
#
# The sea grades follow the National Maritime Board panels: officers (deck and
# engineer certificates), ratings (deck and engine) and catering (cooks and
# stewards). The 1928 assistant-steward rate, GBP 8 5s a month against the able
# seaman's GBP 9, is the measured basis for the catering differential.
#
# Ship's officer. 1700-1960 is 1.90 x the rating. That ratio is an assumption:
# the only measured officer point is the 1937 masters' minimum of GBP 22 a
# month, which is 2.57 x the 1936 able seaman and is the top of the officer
# range. 1967 and 1970 are derived gross earnings: the measured basic-rate
# officer/AB ratio (1.524 and 1.397) applied to the measured AB gross. 1973-1980
# are McConville's Maritime Earnings Survey average officer earnings. 1999-2026
# is ASHE ship and hovercraft officers, mean (SOC90 332, SOC2000 3512, SOC2010
# 3513, SOC2020 3512). The 2002 and 2011 ASHE points are classification breaks
# and are not used.
#
# Ship's rating. 18th-century merchant able seaman 25-30s a lunar month (forum
# source, secondary); 1850-1910 National Maritime Board firemen less 10s a
# month for the able-seaman differential; 1912 able seaman 100s a month; the
# 1928-1960 National Maritime Board rates; 1967-1980 McConville gross weekly
# earnings (table 6.4); 1990 and 1992 NES Table 86, gross weekly earnings;
# 1999-2026 ASHE marine and waterways transport operatives, mean (SOC90 880,
# SOC2000/SOC2010/SOC2020 8232). The gross-earnings rows are used from 1967
# because the 1999 ASHE anchor is gross earnings; the basic-rate rows in
# McConville table 6.1 are recorded as the no-overtime alternative.
#
# Ship's catering. 1928 assistant steward GBP 8 5s a month, 0.92 of the able
# seaman. The ratio falls linearly to 0.60 of the rating at 1999 and is held to
# 2026. The 1999-2026 values are derived, not measured: no ship's steward
# series exists, and the ASHE cook and air-travel-assistant rows are shore
# proxies with classification breaks of their own. Every catering point is
# computed from the rating point at the same year.
#
# Basis joins. 1960 is the last basic-rate anchor and 1967 the first
# gross-earnings anchor, so 1960-1967 carries a rate-to-earnings step as well as
# a pay rise. The 1990 and 1992 NES rows are a classification break: 1990 is
# pre-SOC90 with 1,546 in sample, 1992 is SOC90 with 100. The 1990-1992 rise is
# therefore part definition and part pay, and is not read as a wage increase.
#
# Aircraft flight deck. 1909 estimate (the 1924 scale deflated by the general
# index); 1924 Imperial Airways scale midpoint; 1954 National Joint Council
# scale midpoint; 1969 BOAC all-pilot proxy; 1980 derived by linear real growth
# between 1969 and 1999; 1999-2026 ASHE aircraft pilots and flight engineers,
# median (SOC90 331, SOC2000/SOC2010 3512). 2021-2023 are omitted: SOC2020 3511
# merges air traffic controllers into the pilot code, and the 2022 median is
# not comparable with 2020.
#
# Cabin crew. 1930 estimate at 0.167 of the flight deck (the measured 1965
# ratio); 1965 BOAC entrant GBP 584 a year; 1980 derived by linear real growth;
# 1999-2026 0.27 of the flight deck, the median measured cabin/pilot ratio over
# the ASHE years. The ASHE air-travel-assistant series includes ground
# passenger staff and is unstable in 2024-2025, so the ratio is used.
#
# 2030-2050 hold 2026 real pay and rise with the general index, as for 0-6.
